Mitch Kapor and John Barlow,


I think it is time to start a Computers and Academic Freedom Project.

At least once a month, someone posts a plea for help from some
university.  The most common complaint is the banning of newsgroups
based on context (usually alt.sex). A while back, one university
employee didn't ban newsgroups directly, put rather set the newsgroup
expiration time so that reading groups such as alt.sex would be
difficult.  In a very recent case, employees of a university killed a
newsgroup note that a student had posted because they said the
information in the note could be misused. In other cases, the
employees of the university may be reading e-mail or looking through
user files. This may happen with or without some prior notice that
e-mail and files are fair game.

In many of these cases the legality of the suppression is unclear. It
may depend on user expectation, prior announcements, and whether the
university is public or private.

The legality is also, however, irreleveent. The duty of the University
is not to supress everything it legally can; rather it is to support the
free and open investigation and expression of ideas. This is the ideal
of academic freedom. In this role, the University acts a model of how
the wider world should be. (In the world of computer, universities
are perhaps the most important model of how things should be).

I would like to help start a project on Computers and Academic Freedom.
The short term goals would be
  1) document cases of computer-related academic suppression
  2) draft a Statement on Computers and Academic Freedom
  3) offer information that may be of help to people fighting for
        academic freedom on computers

Long term goals:
   1) Have the the Statement endorsed by interested groups (such as
      the American Association of University Professors and the
      American and the Canadian Library Associations)
   2) Have the principles of the Statement incorporated into the
      academic code of universities and colleges.
   3) Have the principles adopted by nonacademic groups and organizations.

What I have done so far:
   1) I've collected relevant related statements, such as the
       Freedom to Read statements from the American and the Canadian
       Library Associations, the Statement on Rights and Freedoms of
       Student from the American Association of University Professors,
      etc.
   2) Looked though my archive of e-mail and netnews and found 14
      possible cases of academic suppression.

What I need next:
